Subject: [PATCH 3/3] AVBufferPool and AVRefStructPool entries as Sublet lifetimes
Upstream: ffmpeg 9.0.1
Upstream-Archive: https://ffmpeg.org/releases/ffmpeg-9.0.1.tar.xz
Upstream-SHA256: cf38e0e28c7e5605942c4a77755349b0145804a397af37eb1fb4c77cb237f635
Build-Variants: protected Capstone process (capstone-application, FFPOOL_SUBLET=ON)
Depends-On: ffmpeg-9.0.1-0001-libavutil-pool-event-instrumentation.patch
Apply-From: upstream source root, with patch --batch --forward --fuzz=0 -p1

Every buffer or object a pool hands out is its own allocation, so it
already has a lifetime: the one the system allocator gave it. Returning it
to the pool renews that lifetime (CRENEW), so every copy of the user's
pointer dies before the entry is reissued, and the pool continues with the
renewed pointer. The pools keep upstream's allocation and add no field.
Each entry is freed at the pool's end through that pointer, which names
the allocation's current lifetime.

# AVBufferPool. pool_release_buffer renews the entry's storage and keeps
# the result in BufferPoolEntry.data. av_buffer_pool_get makes the next
# AVBufferRef from it, and buffer_pool_flush frees the storage through it,
# as upstream does.
#
# AVRefStructPool. A refstruct object's RefCount header sits in front of it
# in the same allocation, and unref finds the header from the user's
# pointer. pool_return_entry renews through that pointer and continues with
# the result: the free list holds it and pool_free_entry frees through it.
# Objects allocated outside a pool are untouched.
#
# What this relies on. Each entry is one allocation of the system
# allocator: patch 0001 refuses every buffer allocator but av_buffer_alloc
# and av_buffer_allocz, and a refstruct entry always comes from av_malloc.
# The system allocator gives every object a lifetime of its own, as the
# virtual heap does with lifetimes on. Without that, the pointer names the
# lifetime of something larger, and renewing it would end that.
diff --git a/libavutil/buffer.c b/libavutil/buffer.c
--- a/libavutil/buffer.c
+++ b/libavutil/buffer.c
@@ -362,6 +362,10 @@ static void pool_release_buffer(void *opaque, uint8_t *data)
     AVBufferPool *pool = buf->pool;
     uint64_t call = ff2_begin(FF2_RETURN, FF2_BUFFER, pool, 0, 0, data);
 
+    /* The entry is its own allocation. Renewing its lifetime (CRENEW) ends
+     * every copy of the user's pointer before the entry is reissued. */
+    buf->data = capstone_cap_renew(buf->data);
+
     ff_mutex_lock(&pool->mutex);
     buf->next = pool->pool;
     pool->pool = buf;
diff --git a/libavutil/buffer_internal.h b/libavutil/buffer_internal.h
--- a/libavutil/buffer_internal.h
+++ b/libavutil/buffer_internal.h
@@ -25,6 +25,8 @@
 #include "buffer.h"
 #include "thread.h"
 
+#include <capstone/capability.h>
+
 /**
  * The buffer was av_realloc()ed, so it is reallocatable.
  */
diff --git a/libavutil/refstruct.c b/libavutil/refstruct.c
--- a/libavutil/refstruct.c
+++ b/libavutil/refstruct.c
@@ -21,6 +21,7 @@
 #include <string.h>
 
 #include "refstruct.h"
+#include "buffer_internal.h"
 
 #include "avassert.h"
 #include "error.h"
@@ -246,7 +247,9 @@ static void pool_free_entry(AVRefStructPool *pool, RefCount *ref)
 
 static void pool_return_entry(void *ref_)
 {
-    RefCount *ref = ref_;
+    /* The entry is its own allocation. Renewing its lifetime (CRENEW) ends
+     * every copy of the user's pointer before the entry is reissued. */
+    RefCount *ref = capstone_cap_renew(ref_);
     AVRefStructPool *pool = ref->opaque.nc;
 
     ff_mutex_lock(&pool->mutex);
